2026-08-29T20:06:19.148515Z","explanation":"An official oral-argument transcript was verified.","status":"argued"}],"case_status":"argued","dek":"The Patent and Trademark Office argues that Booking.com is a generic term. Booking.com claims the full phrase identifies its specific brand.","dek_sources":[{"evidence_type":"Transcript","label":"Official Supreme Court Transcript — file page 38, lines 55-55","official_url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/oral_arguments/argument_transcripts/2019/19-46_bq7d.pdf","page_label":"file page 38, lines 55-55"},{"evidence_type":"Transcript","label":"Official Supreme Court Transcript — file page 4, lines 25-41","official_url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/oral_arguments/argument_transcripts/2019/19-46_bq7d.pdf","page_label":"file page 4, lines 25-41"},{"evidence_type":"Transcript","label":"Official Supreme Court Transcript — file page 65, lines 17-17","official_url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/oral_arguments/argument_transcripts/2019/19-46_bq7d.pdf","page_label":"file page 65, lines 17-17"},{"evidence_type":"Transcript","label":"Official Supreme Court Transcript — file page 74, lines 27-7","official_url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/oral_arguments/argument_transcripts/2019/19-46_bq7d.pdf","page_label":"file page 74, lines 27-7"}],"dispositions":[],"latest_court_document_date":"2020-05-04T00:00:00Z","maturity":"official_transcript","official_detail_url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/oral_arguments/argument_transcript/2019","official_disposition_urls":[],"official_docket_url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/19-46.html","primary_docket":"19-46","revisions":[{"correction_note":"Rewritten to the concise citizen-facing editorial standard.","created_at":"2026-08-30T17:31:55.523815Z","maturity":"official_transcript","revision_number":1},{"correction_note":"Migrated to the dated official Court activity contract without model use.","created_at":"2026-09-05T03:22:24Z","maturity":"official_transcript","revision_number":2}],"schema_version":"1.1","sections":[{"heading":"What this case is about","paragraphs":["The dispute centers on whether a generic word remains generic when attached to a domain extension. The PTO seeks to cancel the registration. Booking.com wants to keep its trademark rights."],"sources":[{"evidence_type":"Transcript","label":"Official Supreme Court Transcript — file page 38, lines 55-55","official_url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/oral_arguments/argument_transcripts/2019/19-46_bq7d.pdf","page_label":"file page 38, lines 55-55"},{"evidence_type":"Transcript","label":"Official Supreme Court Transcript — file page 4, lines 25-41","official_url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/oral_arguments/argument_transcripts/2019/19-46_bq7d.pdf","page_label":"file page 4, lines 25-41"},{"evidence_type":"Transcript","label":"Official Supreme Court Transcript — file page 65, lines 17-17","official_url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/oral_arguments/argument_transcripts/2019/19-46_bq7d.pdf","page_label":"file page 65, lines 17-17"},{"evidence_type":"Transcript","label":"Official Supreme Court Transcript — file page 74, lines 27-7","official_url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/oral_arguments/argument_transcripts/2019/19-46_bq7d.pdf","page_label":"file page 74, lines 27-7"}]},{"heading":"How the case got here","paragraphs":["The Supreme Court heard oral argument in this case on May 4, 2020."],"sources":[{"evidence_type":"Transcript","label":"Official Supreme Court Transcript — file page 4, lines 11-23","official_url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/oral_arguments/argument_transcripts/2019/19-46_bq7d.pdf","page_label":"file page 4, lines 11-23"},{"evidence_type":"Transcript","label":"Official Supreme Court Transcript — file page 71, lines 11-31","official_url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/oral_arguments/argument_transcripts/2019/19-46_bq7d.pdf","page_label":"file page 71, lines 11-31"}]},{"heading":"What each side wants","paragraphs":["The PTO wants the Court to cancel the trademark registration.","Booking.com wants the Court to uphold its trademark registration."],"sources":[{"evidence_type":"Transcript","label":"Official Supreme Court Transcript — file page 38, lines 55-55","official_url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/oral_arguments/argument_transcripts/2019/19-46_bq7d.pdf","page_label":"file page 38, lines 55-55"},{"evidence_type":"Transcript","label":"Official Supreme Court Transcript — file page 4, lines 25-41","official_url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/oral_arguments/argument_transcripts/2019/19-46_bq7d.pdf","page_label":"file page 4, lines 25-41"},{"evidence_type":"Transcript","label":"Official Supreme Court Transcript — file page 65, lines 17-17","official_url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/oral_arguments/argument_transcripts/2019/19-46_bq7d.pdf","page_label":"file page 65, lines 17-17"},{"evidence_type":"Transcript","label":"Official Supreme Court Transcript — file page 74, lines 27-7","official_url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/oral_arguments/argument_transcripts/2019/19-46_bq7d.pdf","page_label":"file page 74, lines 27-7"}]},{"heading":"What each side says","paragraphs":["The PTO argues that generic terms cannot become trademarks.","Booking.com argues that the full phrase is descriptive and distinctive."],"sources":[{"evidence_type":"Transcript","label":"Official Supreme Court Transcript — file page 38, lines 55-55","official_url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/oral_arguments/argument_transcripts/2019/19-46_bq7d.pdf","page_label":"file page 38, lines 55-55"},{"evidence_type":"Transcript","label":"Official Supreme Court Transcript — file page 4, lines 25-41","official_url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/oral_arguments/argument_transcripts/2019/19-46_bq7d.pdf","page_label":"file page 4, lines 25-41"},{"evidence_type":"Transcript","label":"Official Supreme Court Transcript — file page 65, lines 17-17","official_url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/oral_arguments/argument_transcripts/2019/19-46_bq7d.pdf","page_label":"file page 65, lines 17